    "md5": "9595453ef0b022d13065c58c09e2e90f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1601,
    "chunk_text": "Crime, poverty and education\n\nJohn Grisham\u2019s novel, The Street Lawyer, gives a vivid picture of poverty in the US and its associated pathos, homelessness, hunger, drugs, prostitution, death due to freezing, and the stark contrast with the superrich and the callousness of the officials and the cruelty of the police. What it does not say is the reason for this incongruity, the failure of the system that produced so much riches and how it could also produce so much wretchedness and penury. A family of five, consisting of a young mother driven to alcohol, drugs and prostitution and four children including an infant, who live in a small car, are frozen to death in the cold of the night. They have been evicted from their home by a construction company which has bought the land to build high rise compartments. In the morgue they are counting the bodies of people who had similarly perished in the night, they too have been similarly evicted.\n\nIt is not just fiction. Officially there were more than 770,000 homeless people in the US in 2024 which represents an 18% increase compared to 2023. Paradoxically the per capita income in the US is about USD 360,000 per annum and there are about 1,000 billionaires; this number also represents an increase from previous years. This shows that the rich are getting richer while the poor are getting poorer in the richest country and this inequality has not been addressed for 250 years. According to neo-liberal economic theory poverty is a personal issue with no one to blame. If a person commits a crime for survival, e. g. theft, he is to be blamed."
  },
