    "md5": "aa05ec711ad6bb780b7350db9003507b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1855,
    "chunk_text": "Tea auction sees sharp decline in premium Western teas\n\nThe auction offerings were marginally higher to the previous week and totalled 6.2 million kilogrammes (M/Kgs), of which 0.7 M/Kgs comprised of reprints and fresh tea offerings of 5.5 M/Kgs.\n\nThe Leafy/Semi-Leafy teas continued to sell well, notwithstanding the price fluctuations within the grades. The Orthodox/Rotovane teas and particularly the teas in the higher price bracket from the Western slopes were substantially easier and often unsold, due to the lack of suitable bids, partially due to the plainer quality.\u00a0 The Ex-Estate offerings totalled 0.92 M/Kgs. The overall quality was relatively uninteresting, with a greater availability of the offerings comprising of the teas in the Below Best and Plainer categories.\n\nThe Westerns in the higher price bracket were sharply lower with many withdrawals, due to a lack of suitable bids. The teas in the Below Best and Plainer categories mostly sold around the previous week\u2019s levels, with the price variances often reflective of the quality.\n\nThe Nuwara Eliyas continued to be sluggish. The Uva/Uda Pussellawas \u2013 Better teas, were firm and marginally easier, whilst the poorer sorts declined quite sharply.\n\nThe current pricing levels reflect a distinct advantage for the BOPF grade, which often realised a premium of Rs.100-150 per kilogramme plus vis-\u00e0-vis their corresponding BOP invoices.\n\nThe High and Medium Grown CTC teas mostly sold around last week\u2019s levels, whilst the corresponding Low Grown types were firm and edged up marginally for selected invoices.\n\nThe shippers to the UK, continent and South Africa appeared to be very selective. Fair interest was seen from Japan, whilst perhaps there was less activity from China. Activity from the shippers to the CIS and Middle East were as usual, although often at lower levels."
  },
