    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/opinion/The-Asian-century-is-here-Sri-Lanka-must-decide-and-define-its-course-Ranil/14-794813",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1784333771.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-07-18-dailyftlk-3f8ee2bf-0004",
    "chunk_index": 4,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "75dc205882faaafb3ce08fc6d6e22030",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1934,
    "chunk_text": "If the twenty-first century belongs to Asia, the latter half of the century is likely to belong increasingly to Africa. Population growth, expanding infrastructure and rising investment are already transforming large parts of the continent. China has recognised this reality and has invested heavily in African transport networks, ports, railways and industrial development.\n\nInstead of focusing only on immediate political debates, we must understand where the world is heading over the next quarter century. Our policies, education system, infrastructure, trade relationships and investment strategies should all be designed with that future in mind.\n\nThe next generation will not inherit the world that my generation entered. It will inherit a far more competitive, technologically advanced and economically interconnected Asia.\n\nWhether Sri Lanka prospers in that environment will depend on the choices we make today.\n\nThe changing world order is only one part of the challenge before us. Equally significant are two forces that will transform every society, every economy and every workplace over the next quarter century: climate change and artificial intelligence.\n\nClimate change is no longer a distant environmental concern. It is becoming an economic issue, a social issue and ultimately a national security issue. By 2050, every country will have to adapt to changing weather patterns, increasing temperatures, water scarcity and higher energy demands.\n\nEven developed countries are beginning to confront these realities. Britain, where air conditioning was once considered unnecessary, is now grappling with rising cooling requirements and the infrastructure needed to support them. Governments everywhere will have to rethink how they manage water resources, electricity generation and urban development. These are not challenges unique to developing countries; they are global challenges that will reshape public policy."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-07-18-dailyftlk-3f8ee2bf",
