    "md5": "423f1def946e02cfec900ee3a27f2a08",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1766,
    "chunk_text": "Ten years after the failed coup attempt of 15 July 2016, Erdo\u00f0an stands as one of the most durable political figures of the twenty-first century. Few leaders have experienced a military uprising, survived it, transformed it into the defining narrative of national politics and then used its aftermath to redesign the architecture of the state. It emerged with a fundamentally different political order.\n\nOne of the enduring ironies of modern Turkish politics is that the movement later accused of masterminding the coup had once been one of the governing coalition\u2019s most valuable partners. During the Justice and Development Party\u2019s (AK Party) first decade in power, followers of the exiled cleric Fethullah G\u00fclen expanded their influence across the police, judiciary and sections of the bureaucracy while helping to weaken the military\u2019s long-standing political dominance through the Ergenekon and Sledgehammer investigations. The alliance collapsed spectacularly after the 2013 corruption investigations targeting ministers and figures close to the presidency, transforming former partners into bitter enemies. The failed coup completed that rupture and furnished the political legitimacy for a purge on a scale unprecedented in the history of the Turkish Republic.\n\nMore than 150,000 public officials were dismissed or suspended, while well over 100,000 people faced detention or imprisonment on allegations of links to the G\u00fclen movement, which Ankara holds responsible for the coup. Entire sections of the judiciary, military, universities and civil service were rebuilt. Critics argued that the purges rapidly extended far beyond those directly implicated in the coup and became a vehicle for replacing institutional pluralism with political loyalty."
  },
