    "chunk_size_bytes": 1957,
    "chunk_text": "More than 150,000 public officials were dismissed or suspended, while well over 100,000 people faced detention or imprisonment on allegations of links to the G\u00fclen movement, which Ankara holds responsible for the coup. Entire sections of the judiciary, military, universities and civil service were rebuilt. Critics argued that the purges rapidly extended far beyond those directly implicated in the coup and became a vehicle for replacing institutional pluralism with political loyalty.\n\nSoner \u00c7a\u00f0aptay, in The New Sultan, describes Erdo\u00f0an as an \u201canti-Atat\u00fcrk Atat\u00fcrk\u201d. The phrase captures the central paradox of his rule. Mustafa Kemal Atat\u00fcrk built a secular republic through centralised state power. Erdo\u00f0an has dismantled much of that secular order by deploying the same instruments of centralised authority. The ideological destination changed. The governing method remained remarkably familiar.\n\nHis first decade in government explains why that narrative proved so resilient. Turkey experienced rapid economic expansion, improved healthcare, modernised infrastructure and a substantial reduction in poverty. As \u00c7a\u00f0aptay acknowledges, Erdo\u00f0an\u2019s record \u201chas many positive elements\u201d, including significant improvements in living standards. These achievements created enduring electoral loyalty among millions of Turks whose lives genuinely improved under AK Party governments.\n\nThe relationship between prosperity and political dominance became increasingly reciprocal. Electoral victories strengthened executive authority. Executive authority facilitated greater influence over institutions. Institutions, in turn, reinforced electoral advantage. Scholars increasingly describe Turkey as a competitive authoritarian system rather than a conventional liberal democracy. Elections remain meaningful and opposition parties continue to govern major municipalities, yet the political playing field has become progressively more uneven."
