    "md5": "ed41c272cacf535fd336d68d503af76e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1829,
    "chunk_text": "Handsome, stylish, swift footed and technical, imperious, his vicious right hook floored many an opponent. In fact, this right hook floored Jercz Adamski of Poland, the reigning European champion in the \u201cFeather Weight class\u201d, at the Rome Olympics in 1960. Adamski who bounced back beat Sumith on points and eventually won the Silver Medal in his weight.\n\nSomewhere in 1962, after playing for University in a Sara Trophy cricket match on the University grounds, I hurriedly made my way to the Police Depot ring (now Field Force Headquarters) to watch the Boxing Nationals. This was my first glimpse of Sumith Liyanage who tamed Corporal Wickramapala of the Army, a reputed boxer of the day, with consummate ease.\n\nHe made a distinct impression with his science, nimble footwork, speed and style. His counter punching against a bold, relentless opponent was a feature to watch. I left the venue fully satisfied after watching the amazing standards in boxing on display. I have seen some competitions in seventies and eighties but hold the view that the standards in the sixties were far superior.\n\nI remember with nostalgia, a few other excellent boxers at this meet, mostly from the Police and Armed Services : Marshall, Hepponstall, Boustead, Hope, Percy Wijesuriya, Ekanayake brothers [Hector and Bertie], Elsworth Pereira, Dharmasiri Weerakoon and CP Jayasuriya.\n\nWhen I joined the police in 1965, CP Jayasuriya, Sumith Liyanage and Dharmasiri Weerakoon were already well settled in the Service. What was evident at the time was the fierce competition among outstanding schoolboy sportsmen to enlist in the police as Sub Inspectors.\n\nThe police in those heady days of the 1960s and seventies was a much admired service.\n\nThis heritage was influenced by exemplary leaders who generally did not succumb to unlawful dictates."
  },
