    "chunk_text": "I am sure that many officers of today are ignorant of names of stalwarts such as Osmund de Silva , Eleric Abeygoonewardene, Cyril Herath [uncompromising with interference], Sidney de Zoysa [DIG], and in sports, of legends such as Sumith Liyanage and Dharmasiri Weerakoon.\n\nSo also was the case with many outstanding sportsmen who then joined the police. They were proud men confident of their leadership skills acquired in the playing fields and boxing rings, inspired to resist interference in their work from above.\n\nA brief sketch of Sumith Liyanage\u2019s record in boxing would merit reflection. He took to boxing like a duck to water at Ananda and Nalanda colleges. Whilst being a schoolboy,\n\nhe won his weight [feather] thrice at national meets. These were outstanding and exceptional achievements. Prior to nationals, he also won his weight in the Stubb Shield competitions for schools\n\nA unique feature of his 12-year boxing career was that he remained undefeated, except once, in Sri Lanka. The National Selection Committee even made a strong case for him to be sent to the\n\nMelbourne Olympics of 1956. After considerable deliberation, they ruled against his participation as he was only 19-years old. From school days to his retirement, he acquired fame for knocking\n\nout opponents at Stubbs Shield, Amateur Boxing Association and National meets.\n\nSumith Liyanage was a household name in most parts of the country due to his ability to dispose of opponents through knockouts. The only defeat he suffered in his 12-year long boxing career was\n\nafter his return from Rome Olympics. Prior to announcing his retirement, he fought Malcolm Bulner, a rising star, and lost on points in a close fought bout."
