    "chunk_text": "Sumith Liyanage was a household name in most parts of the country due to his ability to dispose of opponents through knockouts. The only defeat he suffered in his 12-year long boxing career was\n\nafter his return from Rome Olympics. Prior to announcing his retirement, he fought Malcolm Bulner, a rising star, and lost on points in a close fought bout.\n\nSport today, in contrast to what it wast then, is highly professional with massive financial rewards. Despite benefits, the danger in this mix is amply proved by specific cases where some sportsmen obsessed with money give second place to representing the country.\n\nThe life of Sumith Liyanage, both in his world of boxing and his professional career, were laced with pride, values, ethics and culture. He was therefore the complete or perfect legend in his favourite sport, boxing. He was also a renowned fast bowler in cricket, and a crack shot in pistol and rifle shooting. He had a natural flair for sports with boxing taking centre stage in his\n\nThe United States celebrated\u00a0 250 years of freedom on the Fourth of July in spectacular style\n\nLeave a Reply Cancel replyYour email address will not be published. Required fields are marked *Comment * Name *\n\nSave my name, email, and website in this browser for the next time I comment."
