    "md5": "1e8a6b54e1c477892835aca2cabc2791",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1933,
    "chunk_text": "England hold off India to seal series\n\nJuly.20 (Mirror Sports) - Ben Duckett hits a record 141 as England post their highest ODI total at Lord\u2019s and beat India by 27 runs to win the series 2-1.\n\nEngland survived a stunning India fightback to beat the visitors by 27 runs at Lord\u2019s and seal a 2-1 series victory.\n\nBen Duckett smashed 141 from 135 balls as England posted 387-3 \u2014 their highest ODI total at the famous ground.\n\nDuckett and Jacob Bethell gave England a flying start, sharing a record 192-run opening partnership. Bethell fell for 91 before Duckett was eventually dismissed after hitting 18 fours and a six.\n\nJoe Root then took control of the closing stages with an unbeaten 74 from 48 balls, while Jos Buttler produced a late burst of power with 41 not out from just 13 deliveries.\n\nRoot's latest half-century was his sixth consecutive score of 50 or more in ODIs.\n\nChasing 388 for victory, India stayed in contention for much of their innings thanks to a superb century from Rohit Sharma.\n\nThe 39-year-old struck 17 fours and five sixes in his 110-ball 138 and shared a 147-run opening partnership with Shubman Gill, who made 77.\n\nBut Rohit's dismissal in the 38th over proved decisive. He was bowled around his legs by Jacob Bethell, leaving India needing to maintain an increasingly demanding run rate.\n\nVirat Kohli attempted to keep the chase alive with 74, but India's middle order struggled to build momentum. Kohli was eventually caught in the covers off Sam Curran, effectively ending India's hopes.\n\nIndia's lower order continued to find boundaries, but the target proved too great as they finished on 360-7.\n\nEngland captain Harry Brook won his third successive toss of the series and saw his openers produce a record-breaking start.\n\nDuckett and Bethell had endured difficult outings earlier in the series, but both were in complete control at Lord\u2019s as India's bowlers struggled to find their length."
  },
